  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Thursday/...\nIssued 221 AM MST THU NOV 26 2026\nAn active and unsettled pattern will continue through next week, \nwith moist southwest flow continuing valley rain and mountain snow \nthrough Tuesday. A closed low will help split the upper level flow \non Tuesday, bringing a deep upper level trough over the region and \npushing much colder air into the region by Tuesday and Wednesday. \nThis will lower snow levels to 4000-5000 feet MSL by Tuesday. The \nclosed low and bulk of precipitation will likely stay to the south \nof our region on Wednesday, with the best chance of showers \nover the southern tier of our CWA near the Nevada border. As \nthis low swings east, a quick building ridge of high pressure \nwill bring warm and dry conditions late Wednesday into Thursday.\nAnother weak shortwave trough will move over Washington State \nand northern Idaho by midweek, bringing another chance of light \nrain and mountain snow late Thursday into Friday. Uncertainty \nremains on the timing and exact location of these successive \nshortwave troughs, so precipitation chances remain around 15-40%\non Wednesday and Thursday. Temperatures will remain around 4-8 \ndegrees above normal through the long term period.",
